1
00:00:00,612 --> 00:00:02,741
Meet Maria. She sells all day.

2
00:00:02,961 --> 00:00:06,033
Customers come, and payment notifications keep ringing.

3
00:00:08,606 --> 00:00:13,026
Except her business money shares an account with groceries and supplier bills.

4
00:00:13,246 --> 00:00:16,464
Some sales go into a notebook; others vanish in the rush.

5
00:00:16,684 --> 00:00:18,066
What can she actually spend?

6
00:00:20,536 --> 00:00:25,576
Sebrae reports that 61% pay business expenses with personal accounts,

7
00:00:25,796 --> 00:00:30,463
25% use notebooks, and 10% don't track finances at all.

8
00:00:32,611 --> 00:00:35,039
But there's another invisible asset: trust.

9
00:00:37,892 --> 00:00:40,608
João buys supplies and agrees to pay later.

10
00:00:40,828 --> 00:00:42,136
His neighbor interrupts:

11
00:00:42,356 --> 00:00:43,448
NEIGHBOR: You can trust João!

12
00:00:48,012 --> 00:00:49,812
Around here, everyone knows him.

13
00:00:50,032 --> 00:00:51,980
But in a new market, he's a stranger.

14
00:00:52,200 --> 00:00:54,767
How can the next supplier see the commitments he honored?

15
00:00:59,767 --> 00:01:03,008
His relationships mattered. But their history didn't travel.

16
00:01:03,228 --> 00:01:04,153
Until GIRO.

17
00:01:07,502 --> 00:01:11,607
GIRO organizes recorded income, reserves, and obligations.

18
00:01:11,827 --> 00:01:13,882
Maria registers a thousand reais.

19
00:01:14,102 --> 00:01:18,653
Four hundred are reserved; six hundred remain available based on her records.

20
00:01:20,849 --> 00:01:22,433
Planning is her own decision.

21
00:01:22,653 --> 00:01:28,691
A shared commitment is different: João and his supplier review and accept the same commercial terms.

22
00:01:32,615 --> 00:01:36,339
They agree the supplier is owed twenty percent of registered sales.

23
00:01:36,559 --> 00:01:42,044
Later, evidence and appropriate confirmations document whether the obligation was fulfilled.

24
00:01:42,264 --> 00:01:44,015
João cannot certify it alone.

25
00:01:46,700 --> 00:01:54,380
Now GIRO Confiança shows commitments fulfilled on time, completed after renegotiation, active, and finalized.

26
00:01:54,600 --> 00:01:57,389
Not a credit score. Not a guarantee of honesty.

27
00:01:57,609 --> 00:01:59,531
A history with traceable records.

28
00:02:02,808 --> 00:02:05,386
In a new market, João shares selected entries.

29
00:02:05,606 --> 00:02:09,221
JOÃO: Don't just take my word. Check the agreements I fulfilled.

30
00:02:09,441 --> 00:02:11,924
These records can be verified beyond GIRO.

31
00:02:15,728 --> 00:02:17,950
JOÃO: But what if GIRO changes my history?

32
00:02:18,170 --> 00:02:19,661
That's why Solana matters.

33
00:02:19,881 --> 00:02:25,222
Minimal proofs of accepted agreements and confirmed outcomes can be checked independently,

34
00:02:25,442 --> 00:02:27,375
while private contracts stay off-chain.

35
00:02:30,341 --> 00:02:34,090
Solana supports low-cost transactions and programmable payment rules.

36
00:02:34,310 --> 00:02:37,055
Future compatible payments could be split automatically.

37
00:02:37,275 --> 00:02:39,569
It verifies records—not fruit deliveries.

38
00:02:40,362 --> 00:02:46,184
We're four Brazilian women across mathematics, software development, marketing, and graphic design.

39
00:02:46,404 --> 00:02:50,420
Our pilot: three suppliers and twelve of their vendors, in three markets.

40
00:02:50,640 --> 00:02:54,448
Free for vendors, with better terms for those with a GIRO history.

41
00:02:55,437 --> 00:02:57,967
Money organized today, trust for tomorrow.

42
00:02:58,187 --> 00:02:59,024
That's GIRO.
